From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 317/488] soh: name the 9 address-named symbols defined in
 z_door_warp1.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 9 are defined in soh/src/overlays/actors/ovl_Door_Warp1/z_door_warp1.c.

  * func_80999214 -> DoorWarp1_BlueCrystalFadeToBlack
    deduced, confidence HIGH: fades the crystal in while driving the
    environment's ambient, fog and light colours to black over forty frames,
    then hands off to the wait for the boss-clear fanfare.

  * func_80999348 -> DoorWarp1_BlueCrystalStartFanfare
    deduced, confidence HIGH: holds the player in place until the timer runs
    out, then queues NA_BGM_BOSS_CLEAR and arms the 200-frame wait.

  * func_809995D4 -> DoorWarp1_BlueCrystalMoveIntoPlace
    deduced, confidence HIGH: once that wait ends it relocates the warp to
    its fixed chamber coordinates and re-aims both point lights at the new
    position.

  * func_809998A4 -> DoorWarp1_FadeOut
    deduced, confidence HIGH: fades the light ray and the warp body away,
    looping the warp-hole sfx until they reach zero.

  * func_80999EE0 -> DoorWarp1_RutoWarpStartTalk
    deduced, confidence HIGH: sets up the sub-camera framing Ruto and the
    warp and opens her farewell textbox.

  * func_80999FE4 -> DoorWarp1_RutoWarpWaitTalk
    deduced, confidence HIGH: waits for that textbox to close, plays the
    warp sfx, restores the main camera and hands off to
    DoorWarp1_RutoWarpOut.

  * func_8099A3A4 -> DoorWarp1_AdultWarpAppear
    deduced, confidence HIGH: grows the adult dungeon warp in, fading the
    light ray and warp body up to full and expanding its scale until it
    reaches full size, then hands off to DoorWarp1_AdultWarpIdle.

  * func_8099B020 -> DoorWarp1_HalfBrightIdle
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the WARP_UNK_7 action: holds the warp at half
    alpha, growing its two scale factors once that alpha is reached, and
    loops the warp-hole sfx forever.

  * func_8099A508 -> DoorWarp1_AdultWarpTrigger
    deduced, confidence HIGH: after a twenty-frame delay it plays the warp
    sfx and starts the crystal animation, then hands off to the
    already-named DoorWarp1_AdultWarpOut that carries the player away.

